=== Preservation and Transmission ===
Matsumae Kagura was designated as an {{ill|Important Intangible Folk Cultural Property|ja|重要無形民俗文化財|12=draft|12=draft}} on March 8, {{ill|2018|ja|2018th year|12=draft|12=draft}}.<ref name=":0" /> It is being preserved and transmitted under the auspices of the Matsumae Kagura Hokkaido United Preservation Association, together with the Hakodate United Preservation Association, the Otaru Block Preservation Association, the Matsumae Block United Preservation Association, and the Fukushima Town Matsumae Kagura Preservation Association.<ref name=":9">{{Cite web |url=https://www.siomigaoka.com/matumaekagura |title=Matsumae Kagura (Matsumae Kagura Otaru Preservation Association) |access-date=2024-11-29 |publisher=潮見ヶ岡神社}}</ref>

In {{ill|1958|ja|1958th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Showa 33), 12 performers were designated as holders of Hokkaido's intangible cultural properties, with 4 designated in each of the three blocks (Otaru, Fukushima, and Matsumae).<ref name=":10">{{Cite web |url=https://yokisourou.com/kagura-history/ |title=About Matsumae Kagura |access-date=2024-11-29 |publisher=良き候北加伊道}}</ref> In {{ill|1968|ja|1968th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Showa 43), a statewide organization called the "Matsumae Kagura United Preservation Association" was established, and subsequently in {{ill|1995|ja|1995th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Heisei 7) it was selected by the national government as a "folk cultural material for which measures for documentation should be taken." To further consolidate efforts toward designation as a national intangible cultural property, the Matsumae Kagura Preservation Association was dissolved in {{ill|1999|ja|1999th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Heisei 11) and replaced by the "Hokkaido Matsumae Kagura Preservation Association" (an umbrella organization comprising the Hakodate, Matsumae Block, Otaru Block, and Fukushima Town preservation associations).<ref name=":10" /> In {{ill|2008|ja|2008th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Heisei 20), following the death of the last designated holder, Osamu Kimura, the designation under Hokkaido's system was cancelled; however, in June of that year a new designation was granted to the Hokkaido Matsumae Kagura United Preservation Association (covering 4 blocks). Finally, in {{ill|2018|ja|2018th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Heisei 30) it was designated as a national {{ill|Important Intangible Folk Cultural Property|ja|重要無形民俗文化財|12=draft|12=draft}}.<ref name=":10" />

Below is the timeline of events leading up to its national designation:<ref name=":9" /><ref name=":10" />
* {{ill|1958|ja|1958th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Showa 33) – Designated as the first Hokkaido intangible cultural property, with 12 holders recognized.
* {{ill|1995|ja|1995th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Heisei 7) – Selected by the national government as a "folk cultural material for which measures for documentation should be taken" (documentary selection).
* {{ill|1999|ja|1999th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Heisei 11) – The Matsumae Kagura United Preservation Association was formed in anticipation of national designation as an Important Intangible Folk Cultural Property.
* {{ill|2008|ja|2008th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Heisei 20) – Following the death of the last holder, the Hokkaido designation was cancelled; subsequently, it was designated as a Hokkaido intangible folk cultural property with the preservation associations and their four subordinate groups clearly stated.
* {{ill|2018|ja|2018th year|12=draft|12=draft}} (Heisei 30) – Designated as a national {{ill|Important Intangible Folk Cultural Property|ja|重要無形民俗文化財|12=draft|12=draft}}.

